msgid "**Login Methods:** ``services.discourse.login.method.*`` in `meta/services.yml <./meta/services.yml>`__ drives the site settings that `files/ruby/apply_settings.rb <./files/ruby/apply_settings.rb>`__ writes. ``sso`` sets ``openid_connect_enabled``. ``native`` or ``ldap`` turns on ``enable_local_logins``; the LDAP mode keeps that form, so it is a shared form that local accounts can still use. ``ldap`` also sets the LDAP plugin’s ``ldap_auth_enabled``. ``2fa`` is Discourse’s own TOTP and security keys, which Discourse offers exactly when ``enable_local_logins`` is on and cannot switch on its own, so the deploy refuses a ``2fa`` flag that differs from ``native`` or ``ldap``; while it is on, ``login.method.2fa.enforced`` (default ``true``) sets ``enforce_second_factor`` to ``all``, and ``enforced: false`` sets ``no``; while ``2fa`` is off, ``enable_local_logins`` is off too and Discourse answers second-factor setup with NotFound, so any enforcement would lock every account out of the web UI (``enforce_second_factor_on_external_auth`` defaults to true); the deploy then sets ``no``, also over a value an admin chose, and the read-back checks the value on every deploy. Discourse refuses to turn local logins off while an enforcement stands and to enforce while local logins are off, so ``apply_settings.rb`` writes ``no`` before ``enable_local_logins`` and ``all`` after it. ``passkey`` sets ``enable_passkeys``, ``email`` sets ``enable_local_logins_via_email`` (the one-click email link), and ``registration`` sets ``allow_new_registrations``. Under SSO, ``allow_new_registrations`` stays on because Discourse creates the OIDC accounts through it, while ``enable_local_logins`` off keeps local sign-up closed. With SSO on, ``/login`` and ``/signup`` hand over to Keycloak at once."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login Methods:** ``services.listmonk.login.method.*`` in `meta/services.yml <./meta/services.yml>`__ drives `vars/main.yml <./vars/main.yml>`__ and `tasks/02_manager_ops.yml <./tasks/02_manager_ops.yml>`__. ``sso`` sets ``security.oidc.enabled`` (the setting is written while the ``sso`` service is on). ``native`` sets the administrator’s ``password_login``; the accounts the RBAC hook creates never get one. Listmonk renders its password form unconditionally, so while ``sso`` is the only method `files/javascript/oidc-autologin.js <./files/javascript/oidc-autologin.js>`__, stored as ``appearance.public.custom_js``, hides the password form and the reset link and submits the OIDC form; otherwise the setting is emptied. ``2fa`` is Listmonk’s TOTP, which it offers exactly to accounts with ``password_login`` and cannot switch on its own, so the deploy refuses a ``2fa`` flag that differs from ``native``. Listmonk has no setting that requires the second factor, so ``login.method.2fa.enforced`` only offers it here and each account enrolls on its own. Listmonk has no LDAP path, self-registration, passkey or magic link here, so the role declares none of them."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login Methods:** ``services.mediawiki.login.method.*`` in `meta/services.yml <./meta/services.yml>`__ drives the sign-in page through `vars/main.yml <./vars/main.yml>`__. ``sso`` loads PluggableAuth with OpenIDConnect (together with the ``sso`` service) and grants ``autocreateaccount`` so a first OIDC login creates its wiki account. ``native`` sets ``$wgPluggableAuth_EnableLocalLogin`` and whether ``files/shell/realign_admin_password.sh`` gives the administrator a local password. ``registration`` sets ``$wgGroupPermissions['*']['createaccount']``, the local sign-up on Special:CreateAccount. ``$wgPluggableAuth_EnableAutoLogin`` is on while neither ``native`` nor ``registration`` is, so ``sso`` is then the single entry. MediaWiki has no LDAP path, second factor, passkey or magic link here, so the role declares none of them."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login Methods:** ``services.mobilizon.login.method.*`` in `meta/services.yml <./meta/services.yml>`__ drives `templates/env.j2 <./templates/env.j2>`__ and `templates/config.exs.j2 <./templates/config.exs.j2>`__. ``sso`` renders the Keycloak Ueberauth provider (together with the ``sso`` service). ``native`` off sets ``MOBILIZON_INSTANCE_DISABLE_DATABASE_LOGIN=true``, which removes the e-mail and password form. ``registration`` sets ``MOBILIZON_INSTANCE_REGISTRATIONS_OPEN``; Keycloak accounts are created at their first OIDC login regardless of it. The role configures no LDAP backend, and Mobilizon has no second factor, passkey or magic link, so the role declares none of them."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login Methods:** ``services.nextcloud.login.method.*`` in `meta/services.yml <./meta/services.yml>`__ drives the sign-in page. ``sso`` is the OIDC app the ``sso`` service installs (``oidc_login`` with LDAP, ``sociallogin`` without), and ``oidc_login_auto_redirect`` sends ``/login`` straight to Keycloak while ``sso`` is the only method on. ``native`` or ``ldap`` keeps the password form, otherwise the system setting ``hide_login_form`` hides it (`vars/system.yml <./vars/system.yml>`__); the LDAP mode keeps it as a shared form that local accounts can still use, because ``user_ldap`` and the local backend serve the same form. ``2fa`` enables the ``twofactor_totp`` and ``twofactor_nextcloud_notification`` apps. While ``2fa`` is on, every deploy reads ``occ twofactorauth:enforce`` and the enabled apps: with ``login.method.2fa.enforced`` (default ``true``) and ``twofactor_totp`` enabled it switches an unenforced instance ``--on`` and marks the enforcement as its own (``occ config:app:set infinito twofactor_enforced_by_deploy``), so an account without a second factor enrolls one at its next sign-in, and leaves an enforcement an admin already set, groups and exclusions included. With ``enforced: false``, and while ``2fa`` is off (the deploy then disables ``twofactor_totp``), it switches the enforcement ``--off`` only when it still owns it, that is while the marker is set and ``occ`` reports exactly “enforced for all users”, or when no second-factor provider app is enabled at all, because an enforcement without a provider locks every affected account out. Provider apps are the enabled ``twofactor_*`` apps, ``twofactor_totp`` included, and the non-prefixed ones in ``NEXTCLOUD_TWOFACTOR_PROVIDER_APPS`` (``privacyidea``); backup codes do not count. An enforcement an admin set, also one with excluded groups, stays while a provider serves it. The marker goes as soon as the deploy stops enforcing. With enforcement on, Nextcloud refuses the login password on WebDAV, CalDAV, CardDAV and OCS, so desktop, mobile and calendar clients need an app password (Settings, Security, Devices & sessions). The app password the deploy mints for the MCP account is such a device token, which Nextcloud exempts from the second factor. ``passkey`` sets ``auth.webauthn.enabled`` (“Log in with a device”). OIDC account creation stays governed by ``oidc_login_disable_registration``, and Nextcloud has no local self-registration."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login Methods:** ``services.openbao.login.method.*`` in `meta/services.yml <./meta/services.yml>`__ decides which auth mounts exist (`tasks/04_auth_methods.yml <./tasks/04_auth_methods.yml>`__, `tasks/utils/oidc.yml <./tasks/utils/oidc.yml>`__, `tasks/utils/userpass.yml <./tasks/utils/userpass.yml>`__). ``sso`` sets ``listing_visibility=unauth`` on the ``oidc/`` mount, so the login page opens on the OIDC entry; otherwise the mount is ``hidden``. ``ldap`` enables and configures the ``ldap/`` mount when ``svc-db-openldap`` is deployed and disables it when the method is off, so with SSO on LDAP accounts sign in through the Keycloak federation. ``native`` enables the ``userpass`` mount described below. OpenBao offers no self-registration and no passkey, and its login MFA is an enforcement policy rather than a factor users opt into, so the role declares none of them."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login Methods:** `services.discourse.login.method.*` in [meta/services.yml](./meta/services.yml) drives the site settings that [files/ruby/apply_settings.rb](./files/ruby/apply_settings.rb) writes. `sso` sets `openid_connect_enabled`. `native` or `ldap` turns on `enable_local_logins`; the LDAP mode keeps that form, so it is a shared form that local accounts can still use. `ldap` also sets the LDAP plugin's `ldap_auth_enabled`. `2fa` is Discourse's own TOTP and security keys, which Discourse offers exactly when `enable_local_logins` is on and cannot switch on its own, so the deploy refuses a `2fa` flag that differs from `native` or `ldap`; while it is on, `login.method.2fa.enforced` (default `true`) sets `enforce_second_factor` to `all`, and `enforced: false` sets `no`; while `2fa` is off, `enable_local_logins` is off too and Discourse answers second-factor setup with NotFound, so any enforcement would lock every account out of the web UI (`enforce_second_factor_on_external_auth` defaults to true); the deploy then sets `no`, also over a value an admin chose, and the read-back checks the value on every deploy. Discourse refuses to turn local logins off while an enforcement stands and to enforce while local logins are off, so `apply_settings.rb` writes `no` before `enable_local_logins` and `all` after it. `passkey` sets `enable_passkeys`, `email` sets `enable_local_logins_via_email` (the one-click email link), and `registration` sets `allow_new_registrations`. Under SSO, `allow_new_registrations` stays on because Discourse creates the OIDC accounts through it, while `enable_local_logins` off keeps local sign-up closed. With SSO on, `/login` and `/signup` hand over to Keycloak at once."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login Methods:** `services.listmonk.login.method.*` in [meta/services.yml](./meta/services.yml) drives [vars/main.yml](./vars/main.yml) and [tasks/02_manager_ops.yml](./tasks/02_manager_ops.yml). `sso` sets `security.oidc.enabled` (the setting is written while the `sso` service is on). `native` sets the administrator's `password_login`; the accounts the RBAC hook creates never get one. Listmonk renders its password form unconditionally, so while `sso` is the only method [files/javascript/oidc-autologin.js](./files/javascript/oidc-autologin.js), stored as `appearance.public.custom_js`, hides the password form and the reset link and submits the OIDC form; otherwise the setting is emptied. `2fa` is Listmonk's TOTP, which it offers exactly to accounts with `password_login` and cannot switch on its own, so the deploy refuses a `2fa` flag that differs from `native`. Listmonk has no setting that requires the second factor, so `login.method.2fa.enforced` only offers it here and each account enrolls on its own. Listmonk has no LDAP path, self-registration, passkey or magic link here, so the role declares none of them."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login Methods:** `services.mediawiki.login.method.*` in [meta/services.yml](./meta/services.yml) drives the sign-in page through [vars/main.yml](./vars/main.yml). `sso` loads PluggableAuth with OpenIDConnect (together with the `sso` service) and grants `autocreateaccount` so a first OIDC login creates its wiki account. `native` sets `$wgPluggableAuth_EnableLocalLogin` and whether `files/shell/realign_admin_password.sh` gives the administrator a local password. `registration` sets `$wgGroupPermissions['*']['createaccount']`, the local sign-up on Special:CreateAccount. `$wgPluggableAuth_EnableAutoLogin` is on while neither `native` nor `registration` is, so `sso` is then the single entry. MediaWiki has no LDAP path, second factor, passkey or magic link here, so the role declares none of them."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login Methods:** `services.mobilizon.login.method.*` in [meta/services.yml](./meta/services.yml) drives [templates/env.j2](./templates/env.j2) and [templates/config.exs.j2](./templates/config.exs.j2). `sso` renders the Keycloak Ueberauth provider (together with the `sso` service). `native` off sets `MOBILIZON_INSTANCE_DISABLE_DATABASE_LOGIN=true`, which removes the e-mail and password form. `registration` sets `MOBILIZON_INSTANCE_REGISTRATIONS_OPEN`; Keycloak accounts are created at their first OIDC login regardless of it. The role configures no LDAP backend, and Mobilizon has no second factor, passkey or magic link, so the role declares none of them."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login Methods:** `services.nextcloud.login.method.*` in [meta/services.yml](./meta/services.yml) drives the sign-in page. `sso` is the OIDC app the `sso` service installs (`oidc_login` with LDAP, `sociallogin` without), and `oidc_login_auto_redirect` sends `/login` straight to Keycloak while `sso` is the only method on. `native` or `ldap` keeps the password form, otherwise the system setting `hide_login_form` hides it ([vars/system.yml](./vars/system.yml)); the LDAP mode keeps it as a shared form that local accounts can still use, because `user_ldap` and the local backend serve the same form. `2fa` enables the `twofactor_totp` and `twofactor_nextcloud_notification` apps. While `2fa` is on, every deploy reads `occ twofactorauth:enforce` and the enabled apps: with `login.method.2fa.enforced` (default `true`) and `twofactor_totp` enabled it switches an unenforced instance `--on` and marks the enforcement as its own (`occ config:app:set infinito twofactor_enforced_by_deploy`), so an account without a second factor enrolls one at its next sign-in, and leaves an enforcement an admin already set, groups and exclusions included. With `enforced: false`, and while `2fa` is off (the deploy then disables `twofactor_totp`), it switches the enforcement `--off` only when it still owns it, that is while the marker is set and `occ` reports exactly \"enforced for all users\", or when no second-factor provider app is enabled at all, because an enforcement without a provider locks every affected account out. Provider apps are the enabled `twofactor_*` apps, `twofactor_totp` included, and the non-prefixed ones in `NEXTCLOUD_TWOFACTOR_PROVIDER_APPS` (`privacyidea`); backup codes do not count. An enforcement an admin set, also one with excluded groups, stays while a provider serves it. The marker goes as soon as the deploy stops enforcing. With enforcement on, Nextcloud refuses the login password on WebDAV, CalDAV, CardDAV and OCS, so desktop, mobile and calendar clients need an app password (Settings, Security, Devices & sessions). The app password the deploy mints for the MCP account is such a device token, which Nextcloud exempts from the second factor. `passkey` sets `auth.webauthn.enabled` (\"Log in with a device\"). OIDC account creation stays governed by `oidc_login_disable_registration`, and Nextcloud has no local self-registration."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login Methods:** `services.openbao.login.method.*` in [meta/services.yml](./meta/services.yml) decides which auth mounts exist ([tasks/04_auth_methods.yml](./tasks/04_auth_methods.yml), [tasks/utils/oidc.yml](./tasks/utils/oidc.yml), [tasks/utils/userpass.yml](./tasks/utils/userpass.yml)). `sso` sets `listing_visibility=unauth` on the `oidc/` mount, so the login page opens on the OIDC entry; otherwise the mount is `hidden`. `ldap` enables and configures the `ldap/` mount when `svc-db-openldap` is deployed and disables it when the method is off, so with SSO on LDAP accounts sign in through the Keycloak federation. `native` enables the `userpass` mount described below. OpenBao offers no self-registration and no passkey, and its login MFA is an enforcement policy rather than a factor users opt into, so the role declares none of them."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods**: SSO only. The dashboard keeps no accounts of its own, so its one sign-in entry is the Keycloak `Login` link that `oidc.js` renders while `services.sso.enabled` is true; there is no local form, no registration and no break-glass account to protect, so `meta/services.yml` marks the `sso` key `nocheck: login-methods` instead of declaring a `login.method` block."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Login methods**: SSO only. The dashboard keeps no accounts of its own, so its one sign-in entry is the Keycloak ``Login`` link that ``oidc.js`` renders while ``services.sso.enabled`` is true; there is no local form, no registration and no break-glass account to protect, so ``meta/services.yml`` marks the ``sso`` key ``nocheck: login-methods`` instead of declaring a ``login.method`` block."
msgstr ""

